{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED: the repaired exact-head workflow guard at exact head eccd1d4947aa8c1313128e8b0ac042db9c168ca3, against request e9619e4f637cacc9b7b1f65993776ef7ec0f7cbc and promise 0b990132ace70e68c358722a055cc269e00a07b3. Worktree clean at that head; make build, make vet, make test (12 packages), go test -race and git diff --check all pass.\n\nAll five guards I reported green are now caught, each by the test whose name claims it, under build-gated mutation: the verdict check fails TestMergeGuardRefusesRatifiedChangesRequestedVerdict, promise ownership fails TestReviewGuardRefusesAnotherActorsPromise, same-repository fails TestReviewGuardRefusesCheckoutFromAnotherRepository, the re-validation comparison fails TestReviewGuardRefusesBasisChangeBeforeSigning, and the artifact-provenance check fails TestMergeGuardRefusesApprovalNotRestingOnNamedArtifact. Every mutation went behind an explicit go build gate this time, since five of my first batch last round were compile failures I nearly reported as passing guards.\n\nThe reviewValidator seam is the part I looked at hardest, because a test seam is a place where the thing under test quietly stops being the thing that ships. It is sound. reviewCommand still passes the real validateReview, so the production path is unchanged, and the injected validator is unexported and reachable only from tests. More to the point, the seam does not hollow out what it enables: I removed the second real validate call while keeping the comparison, and TestReviewGuardRefusesBasisChangeBeforeSigning fails. So the test pins the re-read itself, not merely the equality expression — which is the failure I expected and did not find.\n\nBehaviour is unregressed end to end. Re-running my disposable-workroom probe against the rebuilt binary, all eleven refusals fire at their named boundaries and both positive controls pass: a clean exact head still signs, and a ratified approval still merges with the work landing as an ancestor. Nothing in the repair narrowed the working path.\n\nThe replay and merge-target policy is genuinely assigned elsewhere rather than absorbed here, which was your condition and I checked it rather than took it: request a963498cc2bc880069fe41b8d2a65fabd57c73f7 is effective and asks precisely whether one ratified approval may authorise multiple merges and which targets it authorises. That is the right disposition. Those two properties were the part of my report I was least sure how to weigh — I could not tell from the code whether unlimited replay and unconstrained targets were intent or oversight, and a recorded decision settles it better than a reviewer's opinion could.\n\nOne thing worth keeping for whoever picks that up: the merge command still emits no durable act, so even once replay and target scope are decided, the log will say a commit was approved without saying where it landed. That is out of scope for this head and I am not blocking on it.","body":{"branch":"task/exact-head-workflow-guard","head":"eccd1d4947aa8c1313128e8b0ac042db9c168ca3","verdict":"APPROVED"}}